Hide the correct option','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 1-40.pdf","supporting_pages":[22,23,24],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":null,"duplicate_sources":[],"parser_version":"manual-review-batch006-2026-09-24","parser_mode":"human_visual_review_batch006","text_method":"manual_visual_plus_context","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,'text',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','low','{"record_id":"8603bcd78bc508dfdeac","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 GIT 1-40.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 1-40.pdf","source_year":2025,"source_page":22,"supporting_pages":[22,23,24],"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Gastroenterology/Surgery","stem":"A 57-year-old man presents to the emergency department with absolute constipation for two days and ongoing vomiting for the past eight hours. On examination, the abdomen is not distended or tender. The rest of the exam is unremarkable except for signs of mild dehydration. Gastrografin studies confirm the presence of small bowel obstruction. Which one of the following is the most common cause of small bowel obstruction?","options":{},"options_mode":"text","source_answer":"A","source_explanation":"Option A Is correct\n\nPost-operative adhesions are the most common\ncause of small bowel obstruction (SBO). The risk\nof developing an obstruction after surgery from\npostoperative adhesions is estimated to be 9%\nwithin the first year after abdominal surgery, 19%\nby 4 years, and 35% by 10 years.\n\nThe incidence of adhesive SBO seems to be\nhigher in open surgeries than in laparoscopic\nsurgeries. The cause for such difference is not\nclear but is likely to be related to the degree of\nmanipulation and trauma to the intraabdominal\n\ntissues.\n\nMalignant tumors are the second most common\ncause of SBO, accounting for about 20% of cases.\nSBO has been described in as many as 42% of\nwomen with ovarian carcinoma and 28% of\n\npatients with colorectal carcinomas.\n\nHernias are the third leading cause of SBO and\naccount for approximately 10% of all cases. Ventral\nand inguinal hernias are most commonly\nassociated with obstruction; however, internal\nhernias, femoral, obturator, and parastomal hernias\n\nalso can contribute to SBO.\n\nOther less common causes of small bowel\n\nobstruction include:\n\ne Strictures e.g., caused by Crohn''s disea\n\n© Small D Quiet 6.\n\nalso can contribute to SBO.\n\nOther less common causes of small bowel\nobstruction include:\n\ne Strictures e.g., caused by Crohn''s disease\n¢ Small bowel tumors\n\ne Trauma\n\ne Intussusception\n\ne Bezoars\n\n¢ Gallstone ileus (pseudo-obstruction)\n\ne Superior mesenteric artery syndrome\n\ne Hypokalemia (pseudo-obstruction)\n\nReference(s) ° UpToDate - Etiologies, clinical ma\nnifestations, and diagnosis of mec\nNanical small bowel obstruction in\nadults\n\n- Medscape - Small-Bowel Obstruc\ntion\n\nHide explanations\n\nHide the correct option","source_answer_status":"source_revealed","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"low","currentness_keywords":[],"likely_visual_question":false,"parser_confidence":"very_high","parser_mode":"human_visual_review_batch006","text_method":"manual_visual_plus_context","parser_version":"manual-review-batch006-2026-09-24","visual_asset":null,"merge_record_ids":[],"manual_review_batch":"006","manual_review_source_key":"","stem_hash":"82b8e88fbcda0bc31170ec95c2ca5016b3451688fc2f19841da7f2295e7d2741"}',0,'v2.9.4','2026-09-24T00:00:00.000Z') ON CONFLICT(record_id) DO UPDATE SET source_file=excluded.source_file,source_page=excluded.source_page,domain=excluded.domain,stem=excluded.stem,options_json=excluded.options_json,source_answer=excluded.source_answer,source_explanation=excluded.source_explanation,provenance_json=excluded.provenance_json,visual_asset=excluded.visual_asset,visual_url=excluded.visual_url,options_mode=excluded.options_mode,curren