A w=','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 41-75.pdf","supporting_pages":[23,24,25],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":null,"duplicate_sources":[],"parser_version":"manual-review-batch006-2026-09-24","parser_mode":"human_visual_review_batch006","text_method":"manual_visual_plus_context","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,'text',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"a18c2f9e5867f6f10f49","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 GIT 41-75.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 41-75.pdf","source_year":2025,"source_page":23,"supporting_pages":[23,24,25],"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Gastroenterology/Surgery","stem":"A 67-year-old man presents to your GP clinic with a complaint of fresh rectal bleeding covering the outer surface of his stool for the past three weeks. He is otherwise in good health. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in evaluation?","options":{},"options_mode":"text","source_answer":"A","source_explanation":"Option A Is correct\n\nRectal bleeding is often caused by benign\nconditions such as hemorrhoids or anal fissures.\nHowever, this assumption may mask serious and\nmore proximal pathologies such as colorectal\ncancer. To avoid missing patients with sinister\nconditions, either as the cause of bleeding or a\nconcomitant problem, all such patients should be\nassessed even if, based on the history, the etiology\niS apparent.\n\nThe approach to patients with rectal bleeding starts\nwith a thorough history followed by a physical\nexam. The physical examination should be\ndirected at identifying possible or definite sources\nof bleeding and finding worrisome distal lesions\nthat may be detectable. A detailed physical\nexamination must include an external inspection of\nthe anus and a digital rectal exam as initial\ninvestigations.\n\nHaving the patient bear down during the\nexamination may induce prolapse of a hemorrhoid\nor bleeding of a superficial lesion. Moreover, office-\nbased anoscopy or proctoscopy (option B) should\nbe performed in patients with acute rectal bleeding\nwhen available. These are simple measures that\ndo not require bowel preparation.\n\nSigmoidoscopy (options C and D) or colonoscop\n\n(option E) is indicated in patients whose initial\n\nassessment suggests more proximal lesions a\nSD\n\ncause.\n\nPDleedindg or a superlicial lesion. IMioreover, OMice-\nbased anoscopy or proctoscopy (option B) should\nbe performed in patients with acute rectal bleeding\nwhen available. These are simple measures that\ndo not require bowel preparation.\n\nSigmoidoscopy (options C and D) or colonoscopy\n(option E) is indicated in patients whose initial\nassessment suggests more proximal lesions as the\ncause.\n\nReference(s) * UpToDate - Approach to minimal\nbright red blood per rectum in adu\nIts\n\nHide the correct option\n\nHide explanations\n\nCopyright ©2021 AMEDEX PTY LTD. All Rights Reserved\n\nA w=","source_answer_status":"source_revealed","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"medium","currentness_keywords":[],"likely_visual_question":false,"parser_confidence":"very_high","parser_mode":"human_visual_review_batch006","text_method":"manual_visual_plus_context","parser_version":"manual-review-batch006-2026-09-24","visual_asset":null,"merge_record_ids":[],"manual_review_batch":"006","manual_review_source_key":"","stem_hash":"ec0ab5cd86d07ee6b8c5c904bd4d43131ad5f63b9393be55f7974c4505765d64"}',0,'v2.9.4','2026-09-24T00:00:00.000Z') ON CONFLICT(record_id) DO UPDATE SET source_file=excluded.source_file,source_page=excluded.source_page,domain=excluded.domain,stem=excluded.stem,options_json=excluded.options_json,source_answer=excluded.source_answer,source_explanation=excluded.source_explanation,provenance_json=excluded.provenance_json,visual_asset=excluded.visual_asset,visual_url=excluded.visual_url,options_mode=excluded