In the assessment of female patients with
primary amenorrhea, two critical questions
Should be answered:
Are secondary sexual characteristics present or
absent?
A physical examination will evaluate secondary
Sexual characteristics such as breast
development, axillary and pubic hair, growth,
etc). Breasts are an endogenous assay for
estrogen. Breast development over the Tanner II
Stage indicates the presence of estrogen and
ovarian function. With breasts above Tannger Il,
the next step is always checking for the
presence or absence of a uterus, uterine
anomalies, vaginal septum or absence of vagina,
imperforated hymen, etc. With no breast
development, hormonal assay including FSH, LH,
TSH, and prolactibe comes first.
Is a uterus present or absent?
A step-by-step approach to primary
amenorrhea is shown in the following
diagram:
